Subject: Re: Is a model still usable if there are highly correlated indicators, low sample, negative eigenvalues?
Message-ID: <gnse3C6YNO4.dh_bv1jABgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <gnse3C6YNO4.ET8rGjAGEAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <gnse3C6YNO4.ET8rGjAGEAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/gnse3C6YNO4/m/dh_bv1jABgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The warning messages are pretty clear.  You cannot trust results when you have more variables than observations.  I'm surprised the model would even run.  Results are already questionable when you estimate more parameters than you have observations, and there are certainly more model parameters than variables (at a minimum, there is one (residual) variance per variable, plus parameters representing relationships between variables).  Furthermore, you have categorical data.  Simulations seem to indicate that even when you have more observations than parameters (and certainly more than variables), estimates still do not stabilize until you have moderately large samples (e.g., N >= 500 or 1000).

http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.859510

http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/10705511.2014.882658

Sorry to say it, but if I were reviewing your paper, I would not even be able to recommend a revision unless you could increase your sample size by an order of magnitude to be able to fit a model this large.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
UvA web page: http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

